@page "/share"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<PageTitle>Share &amp; automate · BevTools</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <p class="bb-overline">Beyond the panel</p>
    <h1>Share &amp; automate</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        What BevTools records can leave the in-app panel: into a window of its own, into the browser's DevTools, into a
        file a colleague opens, and into CI. Every one of them reads the same data through the same redaction, so none
        shows more than the panel does.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Three frontends, one protocol</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        In each .NET runtime BevTools records what happens and answers questions about it (<code>BevToolsProtocol</code>).
        A script on the page, the <em>page hub</em>, keeps one <em>target</em> per runtime that rendered a
        <code>&lt;BevToolsPanel /&gt;</code> - a Blazor Server circuit and the WebAssembly runtime can share a page - and
        three frontends read it:
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="BevTools' three frontends">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Frontend</th><th>Where</th><th>What it is best at</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">In-app panel</td><td>Inside the app, on every host: Server, WebAssembly, Auto, Blazor Hybrid</td><td>Live object inspection, the profiler's batch views, panels that extensions contribute, nothing to install</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Companion window</td><td>⧉ in the panel header: a window of its own, same origin</td><td>Every runtime of the page at once; renders nothing into the app, so on Blazor Server it adds no render batches to the circuit it measures; opens exported sessions offline</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Browser extension</td><td>A <em>Blazor</em> panel in Chromium or Firefox DevTools</td><td>The companion where developers already look; follows the Elements panel's selection (Chromium); no permissions, no content script</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span><RendererStatus /></div>
        <div class="bb-toolbar">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="AskHub">Ask the page hub</button>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="_content/Bit.BevTools/companion.html" target="bit-bevtools-companion" rel="opener">Open the companion window</a>
        </div>
        @if (_targets is { Length: 0 })
        {
            <p class="bb-muted">No BevTools runtime has attached to this page yet: open the panel once.</p>
        }
        else if (_targets is not null)
        {
            <div class="bb-scroll">
                <table class="bb-table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Platform</th><th>Render mode</th><th>Value access</th><th>Capturing</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var t in _targets)
                        {
                            <tr><td>@t.Label</td><td class="bb-nowrap">@t.Platform</td><td class="bb-nowrap">@t.RenderMode</td><td class="bb-nowrap">@(t.Error ?? t.ValueAccess)</td><td>@(t.Error is null ? (t.Capturing ? "yes" : "paused") : "–")</td></tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <em>Ask the page hub</em> does what the companion and the extension do: it lists the targets with
        <code>window.__BIT_BEVTOOLS__.targets()</code> and asks each one <code>hello</code>. The link opens the companion
        the way ⧉ does; a runtime appears in it once its panel has rendered. Set <code>Ui.ShowBadge = false</code> to
        keep the in-app panel out of sight and use only the companion or the extension.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Share what you found</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <strong>About</strong> exports the session three ways, the same as <code>&gt;export</code>, <code>&gt;report</code>
        and <code>&gt;trace</code> in <kbd>Ctrl</kbd><kbd>K</kbd>:
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><em>Export session (JSON)</em>: everything the panels show, in the <code>bit-bevtools/1</code> format. The companion opens it offline (<em>Open file</em>, or drop it on the window), Profiler → <em>Compare…</em> compares against it, and <code>bevtools</code> checks it in CI.</li>
        <li><em>Copy report (Markdown)</em>: the findings, the errors and their groups, the busiest endpoints and the costliest components, ready for an issue.</li>
        <li><em>Export performance trace</em>: the timeline in the Chrome Trace Event format, which <a href="https://ui.perfetto.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ui.perfetto.dev</a> and the Chrome DevTools Performance panel open as they are - render batches with each component's render nested inside, UI events, navigations, requests and interop calls on tracks of their own, and arrows from each event to what it caused. <em>Export performance trace since the profiler baseline</em> in the palette keeps only what happened after the last <em>Reset</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Each contains exactly what the panels show, with the same redaction; browser storage previews are left out. To
        keep a busy app from pushing what you are reading out of the buffers first, <em>pause</em> capture (<em>⏸ Pause</em> in the
        header, or <code>&gt;pause</code>): errors are still collected, and the resume marker says how many renders were
        skipped.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> The browser extension</h3>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c"># from src/Utilities/BevTools</span>
dotnet build Bit.BevTools                    <span class="c"># builds the companion bundle the extension hosts</span>
cd Bit.BevTools.Extension &amp;&amp; node build.mjs   <span class="c"># assembles the unpacked extension in dist/</span></pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Load <code>dist/</code> unpacked (<code>chrome://extensions</code> → <em>Developer mode</em> → <em>Load
        unpacked</em>, or Firefox's <code>about:debugging</code> → <em>Load Temporary Add-on</em>), open DevTools on this
        site and pick the <strong>Blazor</strong> panel. It reaches the page only through DevTools while DevTools is open
        on the tab.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Compare two recordings</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Profiler → <em>Compare…</em> reads an export from before a change and compares each component type with what has
        rendered since: <em>regressed</em>, <em>improved</em>, <em>new</em> or <em>gone</em>, with its renders and time
        before and after, warnings when the two recordings differ in how they were made, and <em>Copy as Markdown</em>.
        Render counts are compared exactly; times only beyond 5 ms and 50 %, because they vary between runs. The
        Products page has a fix to measure this way.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-try">
        <a href="products" class="btn btn-outline">Measure the fix: Products →</a>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Fail a build on what BevTools found</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The <code>bevtools</code> .NET tool (<code>Bit.BevTools.Cli</code>) reads export files - never a running app -
        writes Markdown and exits with <code>1</code> when the check fails (<code>2</code> when an input cannot be read):
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">dotnet tool install --global Bit.BevTools.Cli

<span class="c"># the rules' findings: warnings and errors fail by default</span>
bevtools check checkout.session.json --fail-on warning --ignore render.storm --report "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"

<span class="c"># a component type that renders more often, or clearly longer, than in the baseline</span>
bevtools compare checkout.baseline.json checkout.session.json</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Most rules look back 10 to 60 seconds when they run, so for a recording you will check, set
        <code>o.Diagnostics.EvaluateWhileClosed = true</code>: the rules then run as the app is used, not only while the
        panel is open. In a test, <code>BevSessionCheck.Check(json)</code> and
        <code>BevProfileComparison.Compare(baseline, current)</code> do the same without the tool.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A tool of your own</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The page hub is the one way in for a frontend outside the panel, and it exists only while an enabled runtime is
        attached. A request goes to one runtime and comes back as JSON:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">const</span> hub = window.__BIT_BEVTOOLS__;
<span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">const</span> target <span class="k">of</span> hub.targets()) {
    <span class="k">const</span> hello = <span class="k">await</span> hub.request(target.id, <span class="s">"hello"</span>);
    <span class="c">// snapshot (the bit-bevtools/1 export, by section, or the timeline after an event id),</span>
    <span class="c">// inspect, expand, command and export: documented on BevToolsProtocol</span>
    <span class="k">const</span> snapshot = <span class="k">await</span> hub.request(target.id, <span class="s">"snapshot"</span>, { sections: [<span class="s">"diagnostics"</span>] });
}</pre>
</div>

@code {
    private IJSObjectReference? _module;
    private HubTarget[]? _targets;

    private async Task AskHub()
    {
        _module ??= await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/hub.js");
        _targets = await _module.InvokeAsync<HubTarget[]>("askHub");
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (_module is not null)
        {
            try
            {
                await _module.DisposeAsync();
            }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException)
            {
            }
        }
    }

    private sealed record HubTarget(string Label, string Platform, string RenderMode, string ValueAccess, bool Capturing, string? Error);
}
